  "worked_solution": "Since $x<8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <8 \\\\ x+1 & <9 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1} & <3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}-3 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{x+1}-3$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x<8).$ So, we can divide both sides by $\\sqrt{x+1}-3,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(x-2)}{\\sqrt{x+1}-3} & \\ge \\frac{0}{\\sqrt{x+1}-3} \\\\ x-2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x\\ge 2$ and $x<8.$ So, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le x<8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
